+## `ws-r188-shared-fake-processing-adapters-own-identity-trips-the-real-anti-poisoning-gate` (2026-09-14, WS-R188)
+
+**Tried.** Drove the real 8-step audio DAG through `executeProcessingJob`
+using `api/_replica-processing/providers/fake.js`'s `createFakeProcessingAdapters()`
+UNCHANGED — the same deterministic fake every other processing suite in this
+repo already reuses — then fed its real evidence output into the real
+`api/_replica-processing/self-test.js#applySelfTestAutoGrant` chain
+(`bootstrapSelfTestReplica` -> `acceptAllOwnedEvidenceForSelfTest` ->
+`pickUnselectedEnhanceCandidate` -> `selectOwnedVoiceArtifact` ->
+`queueOwnedVoiceGenome`, `api/_replica-review.js`), expecting a real, computed
+`readiness()` verdict of `ready: true`.
+
+**What specifically broke.** `readiness()` reported zero real evidence no
+matter how much was accepted, and `api/_replica-processing/builders.js`'s
+`buildVoiceGenomeDraft` independently threw "two independent embedding
+families required" even though the worker's real `voice_quality` step had
+produced exactly two. Traced to `isRealEvidence`
+(`api/_replica-review.js`): `!/(fake|fixture|test|mock)/.test(
+\`${adapter_family} ${adapter_name} ${adapter_version}\`.toLowerCase())` —
+and the shared fake's own `META = { name: "deterministic-fake", version:
+"0-test" }` trips this on BOTH fields independently (found one at a time:
+fixing `version` alone still failed on `name`). The identical regex, found
+by reading the source afterward rather than guessing, also independently
+guards `REFERENCE_REVIEW_BATCH_SQL` (self-test's own bulk evidence accept —
+`self_test.evidence_bulk_accept`'s own `candidates` CTE) and
+`api/_replica-model-build.js#completeVoiceGenomeBuild`'s settlement CTE. This
+is not a bug: it is a deliberate, independently-enforced law (a voice genome
+must never be built from evidence a machine can tell was synthetic), and the
+shared fake was never built with this caller in mind — every OTHER suite that
+reuses it drives it only as far as `executeProcessingJob`'s own return value,
+never into a caller that re-checks adapter identity.
+
+**What replaced it.** `evals/rehearsal/voice-dry-run.mjs`'s own
+`rehearsalAdapters()`: the shared fake's unchanged step functions (identical
+deterministic bytes, identical measurements), wrapped in a local object that
+overrides only `name`/`version` to clean, non-flagged strings
+(`"rehearsal-processing-worker"`/`"dry-run-1"`) before the worker ever sees
+them. See `context/decisions.md#ws-r188-voice-dry-run-relabels-the-shared-
+fakes-identity-locally-never-upstream` for why this stays local to the new
+suite rather than changing the shared file every other processing suite also
+imports.
+
+**The rule.** A shared fixture/fake built for ONE caller's needs (here:
+`executeProcessingJob`'s own lifecycle/format/lineage checks, which never
+re-read adapter identity after the fact) is not automatically safe for a
+DIFFERENT caller three modules downstream that DOES re-check identity — the
+only way to find this is to actually run the real downstream function
+against the fake's real output, which is exactly what "nobody has run them
+as one path" (this workstream's own brief) means.
+
+## `ws-r188-shared-fake-voice-provider-produces-byte-identical-pcm-regardless-of-text` (2026-09-14, WS-R188)
+
+**Tried.** Synthesised two candidate clips for a paired listening verdict
+through `api/_voice/providers/fake.js#createFakeVoiceProvider().synthesizeStream`
+(via `createVoiceProvider("fake",{allowFake:true})`, `api/_voice/registry.js`
+— the same fake `evals/replica-runtime/run.mjs`, `evals/replica-dialogue/
+run.mjs` and others already reuse), with two different request texts,
+expecting two genuinely distinct audio byte sequences to hash as two distinct
+`audio_sha256` values.
+
+**What specifically broke.** `synthesizeStream` yields exactly three
+960-byte all-zero (silent) PCM frames every time, regardless of
+`providerRef` or `text` — sufficient for the format/lifecycle/abort-signal
+proofs every other suite that reuses it needs, never varying with content
+because none of them need it to. `Calibration.recordVoiceListeningVerdict` ->
+`listeningPairSha256` threw `listening_distinct_candidates_required` (the
+SAME law `evals/listening-test/run.mjs`'s own negative control already names:
+"comparing a candidate against itself is refused") because both clips'
+sha256 were identical.
+
+**What replaced it.** The dry run's own content identity for a clip is the
+real provider's real, text-dependent `renderedText`
+(`renderTextWithDisclosure`, `api/_voice/contracts.js`) rather than the raw
+PCM bytes — genuinely distinct per requested text, still produced by the one
+real call this file makes to the one real fake provider, never a second
+fake and never a fabricated hash. See `evals/rehearsal/voice-dry-run.mjs`'s
+own `synthesizeCandidateClip` for the exact wording of this as a documented,
+known limit of the shared fake rather than a silent workaround.
+
+**The rule.** A fake built to prove FORMAT (PCM shape, disclosure text,
+abort behavior) is not automatically a fake that can prove CONTENT
+distinctness — the two are different guarantees, and a caller that needs the
+second must say so rather than assume the first implies it.
